            {
              "h": "Early Prepayment of the Installment Facility",
              "p": "Suppose you decide to pay off the entire outstanding loan balance at the conclusion of the fourth month in this illustrative example: you remit the scheduled fourth installment of 1,000,000 VND and simultaneously tender the remaining principal. Because the nominal contract rate is designated as 0%, the contractual debt balance equals exactly eight remaining installments of 1,000,000 VND, totaling 8,000,000 VND. You save zero dollars in interest charges by prepaying, because contractual interest was already zero on paper. Furthermore, depending on the fine print of the consumer credit agreement, the finance company may impose an early prepayment administrative fee ranging from 2% to 5% of the prepaid principal. If you forfeit an upfront cash discount of 600,000 VND to enter the installment program and subsequently settle in month four, your effective annualized borrowing cost escalates significantly to roughly 1.48% per month, compounding to an annual yield exceeding 19%."
            },
            {
              "h": "Customer Returns the Purchased Smartphone",
              "p": "A critical contractual distinction arises when a customer returns the physical smartphone to the retail store after several weeks of usage. If you have already remitted two monthly installments of 1,000,000 VND directly to the finance company, those funds are held on the lender's books rather than in the merchant's cash register. The store cannot simply hand you 12,000,000 VND in physical cash. Instead, the merchant initiates a commercial sales return notice, remitting 11,400,000 VND back to the finance company. The finance company subsequently closes out the credit facility, extinguishes remaining loan obligations, and issues an electronic refund of your 2,000,000 VND in paid installments. If the loan agreement specified non-refundable administrative processing fees, those origination surcharges are forfeited."
            },
            {
              "h": "Missing an Installment Due Date",
              "p": "Consider the operational impact if personal cash flow constraints cause you to miss your fifth installment due date, deferring that payment to settle alongside month six accompanied by a contractual late fee of 150,000 VND. Recalculating the authentic cash flow stream reveals that the borrower's effective monthly borrowing rate escalates from 0.80% to approximately 0.98% per month. Beyond the immediate mathematical penalty, missing a scheduled payment triggers automated delinquency reporting to the national credit information registry. That adverse delinquency classification remains recorded on central credit reporting files for up to five years, directly constraining your borrowing eligibility and driving up risk margins when seeking future mortgage or automotive financing."
            },
            {
              "h": "Disbursement Delays or Settlement Shortfalls",
              "p": "If wholesale disbursement funds fail to arrive in the store's bank account on day T+1 due to interbank clearing disruptions or documentation verification queries, the open Debit balance on Account 1388 remains unresolved on the merchant's balance sheet. At this juncture, the merchant faces institutional counterparty risk: the retail store has already transferred physical possession of the smartphone to the retail buyer, but remains entirely dependent on the creditworthiness and operational stability of the finance company to collect its 11,400,000 VND net cash proceeds."
            }
