              "paras": [
                "Beneath the surface, how does Bank B actually get paid by Bank A? Payment systems do not move high-powered central-bank reserves for every isolated 20 million VND retail order. Doing so would consume unsustainable liquidity, demand immense intraday credit facilities, and cause severe operational gridlock. Instead, the national payment switch runs a continuous multilateral [[net settlement|netting]] engine.",
                "Throughout the business day, millions of individuals and businesses are transferring money back and forth between Bank A and Bank B. Suppose that over a six-hour operating session, Bank A's customers send a cumulative 500 billion VND to Bank B, while Bank B's customers send 470 billion VND to Bank A. At the scheduled 16:30 clearing cut-off, the switch runs an optimization algorithm that offsets 470 billion VND of reciprocal claims.",
                "The switch submits a single multilateral settlement file to the central bank's core banking engine, which debits 30 billion VND from Bank A's reserve deposit account and credits 30 billion VND to Bank B's reserve deposit account. By settling only the net residual difference, the banking system achieves a 97% liquidity compression ratio, settling nearly a trillion VND in commerce with just 30 billion VND in actual reserve money."
              ]
